<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catiline</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 108–62 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Lucius Sergius Catilina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Catiline (Lucius Sergius Catilina) was a Roman patrician politician who, after failing to win the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a>, organised a conspiracy to seize power in 63 BCE. The consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> exposed the plot in his speeches against Catiline, using evidence obtained with the help of envoys of the Allobroges, and several conspirators in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> were executed. Catiline fell in battle at Pistoria in early 62 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catiline.jpg" alt="Catiline" width="296" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cesare Maccari · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cicero_Denounces_Catiline_in_the_Roman_Senate_by_Cesare_Maccari_-_detail_of_Catilina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Catiline belonged to the patrician gens Sergia, which claimed descent from a Trojan companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a>, though no Sergius had been consul since 429 BCE. His father was poor by aristocratic standards. He served under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-pompeius-strabo">Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo</a> in the Social War and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> in 82 BCE. Ancient sources say he grew rich in Sulla's proscriptions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> accused him of killing his brother and two brothers-in-law, among them Marcus Marius Gratidianus. Some modern historians doubt his part in Gratidianus's death and see Cicero's story as political slander. He certainly profited by buying estates cheaply, and by the end of Sulla's dictatorship he was rich.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Catiline was praetor by 68 BCE and governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 67–66. On his return he was refused as a candidate for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a>, and embassies from Africa protested his maladministration. A trial for corruption in 65 BCE ended in acquittal, with former consuls speaking for him. In the elections for 64 BCE, bankrolled by Caesar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a>, he lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-antonius-hybrida">Gaius Antonius Hybrida</a>. A second prosecution for murder in the proscriptions also ended in acquittal. He stood again for 62 BCE and lost to Decimus Junius Silanus and Lucius Licinius Murena, after Cicero had presided at the elections in a cuirass and with a bodyguard.</p><p>Defeated, Catiline gathered dispossessed farmers, ruined Sullan veterans and senators whose careers had stalled, and a rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> began under Gaius Manlius. In October 63 BCE Crassus gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> anonymous letters warning of a massacre, and the senate passed its emergency decree. Cicero denounced Catiline in the senate on 7 November, and Catiline left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, proclaimed himself consul at Faesulae, and was declared a public enemy. Cicero, with the aid of Gallic envoys, caught the conspirators who had stayed in Rome, and they were executed without trial on 5 December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the executions much of Catiline's army melted away, leaving perhaps just over three thousand men, and the Apennine passes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> were blocked. He turned to fight at Pistoria, possibly on 3 January 62 BCE, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-petreius">Marcus Petreius</a> broke his centre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a> wrote that Catiline's men fell where they had stood and that he was found far ahead of them among the slain, still breathing. His name became a byword for villainy, and Sallust made him a symbol of the Republic's moral decline. The evidence is overwhelmingly Ciceronean, and some modern historians have argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> exaggerated or helped to create the conspiracy, though such defences are largely speculative.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Catiline” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Cicero's fourth speech against <b>Catiline</b>, and Cato's demand for severity, the Senate voted to execute five conspirators without trial.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-5">5 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 63 BCE their envoys revealed <b>Catiline's</b> plot to Cicero, but in 61 BCE they rose under Catugnatus and were crushed.</blockquote><a href="/state/allobroges">Allobroges</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Pistoia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-79" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Catiline"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-79">What was here in 79 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
